Take the arbitration queue out of the start path

run-375 names the wall: service 46 is IOCoreSurfaceRoot, its thread prints
'SM enter' and never 'SM locked', and every L1 L2 U1 U2T U3 on the screen is
stamped #44 -- another thread, cycling normally.  So the thread that matters
sleeps inside lockForArbitration's waiting queue and is never woken.

Bring-up starts services one at a time, so the queue is not what keeps them
apart.  Return success before the queue is touched and return from unlock the
same way; the whole arbitration path is then out of the boot until the wakeup
bug is understood.  Revert is this patch, nothing else.
---
diff --git a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
index 0a572fe..21a365e 100644
--- a/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
+++ b/iokit/Kernel/IOService.cpp
@@ -1708,6 +1708,16 @@ IOService::setPMRootDomain( class IOPMrootDomain * rootDomain)
 bool
 IOService::lockForArbitration( bool isSuccessRequired )
 {
+	/* nio: run-375 puts the matching thread to sleep in this function's
+	 * waiting queue and never wakes it.  Service 46, IOCoreSurfaceRoot,
+	 * prints "SM enter" and never "SM locked", while another thread cycles
+	 * L1 L2 U1 U2T U3 -- a wakeup that goes to the wrong element, or to
+	 * none at all.  Bring-up starts services one at a time, so the queue
+	 * is not needed to keep them apart: take the arbitration out of the
+	 * path until the wakeup is understood.  Revert with this patch. */
+	(void)isSuccessRequired;
+	return true;
+
 	bool                          found;
 	bool                          success;
 	ArbitrationLockQueueElement * element;
@@ -1991,6 +2001,9 @@ restart_sleep:                  wait_result = assert_wait( element,
 void
 IOService::unlockForArbitration( void )
 {
+	/* nio: pairs with the early return in lockForArbitration. */
+	return;
+
 	bool                          found;
 	ArbitrationLockQueueElement * element;
 
